<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Namibia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as South West Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Namibia is a country in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, bordering the Atlantic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>. It was the German colony of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> from 1884, where the Herero and Nama genocide took place in 1904–08, and was ruled by South Africa from 1915. After a long liberation war led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> it became independent on 21 March 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/namibia.jpg" alt="Namibia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG by Vzb83 et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Namibia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in United Nations peacekeeping, and former president Martti Ahtisaari received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2008 for his mediation in <b>Namibia</b>, Aceh and Kosovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein, and its rule over <b>South West Africa</b>, which the United Nations later declared illegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Botha then led the campaign that forced the German surrender in <b>South West Africa</b> in July 1915, and South African troops fought in German East Africa under Smuts and on the Western Front, where the South African Brigade suffered heavy losses at the Battle of Delville Wood in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Damara were, with the San, among the first inhabitants of <b>Namibia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Egalitarian foragers who left rock paintings across the region, they were dispossessed and killed in large numbers by colonists from the 18th century, and in Botswana and <b>Namibia</b> they still contend for land and recognition.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Liberation Committee based in Dar es Salaam, channelled money, supplies and diplomatic support to the movements fighting Portuguese rule, white minority rule in Rhodesia and apartheid in South Africa; the last of these struggles ended with <b>Namibia</b>’s independence in 1990 and South Africa’s first democratic elections in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
